"{\"Data Center\":[{\"id\":\"792790325\",\"title\":\"Can I turn Fuel Zones on/off?\",\"text\":\"Home > Data Center FAQs > Can I turn Fuel Zones on/off? Fuel Zones cannot be turned off once they are in use. At this time, you cannot set effective dates such that Fuel Zones are only in effect if the Commence Date is greater than a certain point in time. To inactivate a Fuel Zone so that it cannot be used in new Estimates and Voyages, select its Inactive check box.\"},{\"id\":\"640647407\",\"title\":\"How can I automatically generate Vessel Code upon vessel creation?\",\"text\":\"You may want to enable the system to automatically generate a Vessel Code upon new vessel creation. You can do so by entering a custom expression as the value of configuration flag CFGAutoVesselCode . To set it up with a running alphabetical and numerical order, enter [A-Z] for letters and [0-9] for numbers. In the example below, newly created Vessel Codes will start with A000 and end with Z999 . [image] You can also set a letter or number in the Vessel Code that will remain fixed. For example, Z9[0-9][0-9] will generate Vessel Codes between Z900 and Z999 .\"},{\"id\":\"1274871870\",\"title\":\"How can I set up a Task & Alert rule for Vars-related fields?\",\"text\":\"In order to set up a condition for vars-related fields like Constants Sea, Constants Lakes, Fresh Water, or Others, ensure to pick them up from the Vessel Variables table in the Task and Alerts. [image] Example of the alert. [image][image]\"},{\"id\":\"737116243\",\"title\":\"How can I set up a Task & Alert rule set involving both “And” and “Or” operators?\",\"text\":\"If you're looking to set up a Task & Alert rule set where a task or alert will be triggered once the object (i.e. voyage, cargo, estimate, etc.) meets the following conditions: Condition A is true, AND Either condition B or C is true then the rule set will need to be structured such that conditions B and C are in a group, separate from condition A: [image] To add a group of conditions, click the menu next to the condition below which the new group is to be added, and select Add Group : [image] Once the new group is added, proceed to enter the conditions within the group as per normal.\"},{\"id\":\"679773360\",\"title\":\"How do I set up recurrence options for scheduled tasks?\",\"text\":\"Veson Support can set up a scheduled task to run on a recurring basis using Recurrence Options. The Recurrence options can be set up in the following manner: By time intervals in minutes (e.g., every 120m) At a specific time daily (e.g., 02:00 am) At a specific day and time (e.g., every Monday at 02:00 am) Support can only use one of the above and not a combination. For example, if you would like to run a report every Monday and Thursday, Support will not be able to enter \\\"Monday Thursday 12:00 am\\\". Instead, duplicated tasks will have to be created, one for Monday and another for Thursday. Note: The 'every d' pattern is deprecated.\"},{\"id\":\"1813839920\",\"title\":\"Is there a security right that prevents users from creating new vessels?\",\"text\":\"I am afraid that we do not currently have an access right that prevents users from creating new vessels, however still allowing the user to edit the details of existing vessels. The access rights for the vessel cards can be controlled using the &lsquo;Vessel' and &lsquo;Vessel List' options under Data Center > Quick Links, however this will only provide you with the standard rights options and not the option to prevent vessels from being created. There are a number of steps and menu's users have to go through to create a new vessel, which will assist in preventing a vessel being created in error. If you feel that this is a feature that would greatly improve your use of the system, I would invite you to add this suggestion to our Feature board for voting from the Veson community.\"},{\"id\":\"795706109\",\"title\":\"On Fuel Zones, why doesn’t (cons rate only) compound onto the low-sulfur fuel?\",\"text\":\"Home > Data Center FAQs > On Fuel Zones, why doesn't (cons rate only) compound onto the low-sulfur fuel? On the Fuel Zones form, only one low-sulfur fuel can be selected to consume in an ECA Zone when a (cons rate only) fuel is selected. Keeping this in mind, confirm that the ECA Worldwide Zone is only configured with one low-sulfur fuel.\"},{\"id\":\"4218519763\",\"title\":\"System warning when no consumption line matches the vessel\",\"text\":\"IMOS may prompt a warning message when you update the speed lines in the Speed Consumption Table (Data Center &rarr; Vessel List &rarr; Vessel Details &rarr; Speed Consumption Table). However, the warning does not appear for all vessels. The warning depends on the Ownership type defined for each vessel. IMOS triggers the warning only for OV and TC Ownership because bunker consumption is crucial for these vessel types. For example, in the screenshot below, the vessel is defined as TC. When changes are made in the Speed Consumption Table, IMOS displays the following warning message. [image] For Ownerships other than OV and TC, the warning message will not appear even when changes are made on the Speed Consumption Table. [image] note 80836e8298fb Note: The Speed Laden and Speed Ballast fields at the top of the Consumption tab are not automatically updated when rows are added or removed from the Speed Consumption Table. If you delete a speed from the table, the Speed Laden and Speed Ballast fields retain the old value and the estimate will calculate consumption using an invalid speed. After making any changes to the Speed Consumption Table, always verify that the Speed Laden and Speed Ballast fields reflect a speed that still exists in the table. Update them manually if needed. Note: The Speed Laden and Speed Ballast fields at the top of the Consumption tab are not automatically updated when rows are added or removed from the Speed Consumption Table. If you delete a speed from the table, the Speed Laden and Speed Ballast fields retain the old value and the estimate will calculate consumption using an invalid speed. After making any changes to the Speed Consumption Table, always verify that the Speed Laden and Speed Ballast fields reflect a speed that still exists in the table. Update them manually if needed.\"},{\"id\":\"674791467\",\"title\":\"What is the impact of changing the Delay Property field?\",\"text\":\"The property field in Delay Reasons is used only for reporting purposes. It does not have any impact on the Voyage P&L or to an Estimate . Changing the property field to Planned Maintenance will enable the off-hire to be reported as Planned Maintenance in the On/Off Hire Summary, which is one of the Operations Performance Reports . [image]\"},{\"id\":\"1864040805\",\"title\":\"Why am I unable to add an Attachment on the Time Charter Task screen?\",\"text\":\"The &lsquo;Attachments' on the Time Charter Task screen are associated with the Rule and not the Task instance . The background to this functionality, is for example, there may be Instructions to complete the task, or a Form to fill out, or something that is part of the Task, that you wish to be accessible when ever the Task is generated. To add Attachments, right-click the Task result at the bottom of the Task & Alert Rule Form, select &lsquo;Attachments'. Right click the &lsquo;Attachments' hyperlink on this window to add an Attachment. The Attachment will then be available as read-only, under the Attachments on the Time Charter Task, each time the Task is generated. [image][image] I have added an &lsquo;Example Attachment' as per the below screen shot: [image] This Attachment will now show (as Read only) under the Attachments on the Time Charter Task: [image] Right-clicking the &lsquo;Attachments' hyperlink on the Time Charter Task screen will not provide the option to add any Attachments.\"},{\"id\":\"1807122493\",\"title\":\"Why am I unable to see the full list of logs in the Interface Message List in IMOS?\",\"text\":\"There may be instances where certain interface messages you are looking for seem not to be available or found, even though they should be recorded in the Interface Message List. This usually happens when a user tries to load a large set of data into a list. In such a scenario, IMOS speeds up the load time by loading up a preview set of data, which, oftentimes, is a subset of the entire data set. To inform users of such behavior, you will notice a message at the top of the page stating, \\\"To minimize load time, a list preview appears. To see more records, apply recommended filters marked with <orange triangle>\\\" after the Interface Message List has finished loading. [image] When such a message is displayed, you will need to narrow your search by applying filters with a specific date/time range or filters that are marked with the orange triangle in order to retrieve an accurate and full set of data. Note: This behavior applies to all List views in IMOS.\"},{\"id\":\"844235012\",\"title\":\"Why can't I export lists or reports to PDF?\",\"text\":\"Home > Overview FAQs > Why can't I export lists or reports to PDF? If lists or reports are failing to export PDF, take a quick look at your browser settings to ensure PDFs will download. For example, if you are using Chrome, make sure that PDF files download instead of automatically opening in your browser: [image]\"},{\"id\":\"761037347\",\"title\":\"Why does the Revisions panel show changes that users did not make?\",\"text\":\"The Revisions panel displays a chronological list of revisions (updates made by users or the IMOS Messaging Service) to the current workspace. Users may change only one data point in the workspace but see that the Revisions panel has logged multiple changes. This is a common occurrence if multiple data points are related to one another or if a field influences the value of another (e.g., Changing the vessel's Speed for a voyage leg will force IMOS to calculate and update the Estimated Arrival and Estimated Departure values). If you would like to have further explanation of Revisions or report a potential issue, please reach out to Veson Nautical Support through the Veson Nautical Help Center , and a Support Analyst will be happy to assist.\"},{\"id\":\"3660447885\",\"title\":\"Why do I see the error message \\\"An error occurred while deleting the record. The O-Type record has been used and cannot be deleted.\\\" when trying to delete an Address Type?\",\"text\":\"This error can occur when attempting to delete a record in the Address Book. Even if the entry is not visibly linked to any active records, the system prevents deletion if the Address Type has been used in an invoice at any point. Once an invoice is created, the system permanently retains its association with the Address Type in the database, even if the invoice has been reversed and deleted. As a result, the Address Type cannot be removed. [image] To resolve this issue: Set the existing Address Book record to Inactive - This ensures it is no longer used while preserving historical data. Create a new Address Book record with the correct Type - This allows continued use with the correct configuration. Since modifications to the Type field are not permitted after usage in an invoice, creating a new record is the best approach to maintain data integrity and avoid further system conflicts.\"},{\"id\":\"761037340\",\"title\":\"Why is the drop-down list for a data field highlighting the top result and not the selected value?\",\"text\":\"Home > Overview FAQs Clicking the dropdown arrow is not intended to show the \\\"currently assigned item\\\" in the given form - clicking the dropdown arrow shows users the field autocomplete based on the current text value in the field and will yield the top result, not the selected result.\"}],\"Distances\":[{\"id\":\"1168507752\",\"title\":\"Do I need to run the UpdatePortsNoUNCodes script found in Distances releases?\",\"text\":\"On-Premise clients looking to upgrade their Distances table to the latest release may see the following instructions in the Client Center: \\\"UpdatePortsNoUNCodes only if you don't want any changes done to the UN Codes. Otherwise use tzoneupdate and updateportsV7\\\" The \\\"updatePortsNoUNCodes\\\" script in the distances file is the exact same script as \\\"updatePortsV7\\\", but it will not update the UN codes of the ports. Some clients add their own UN codes in their ports list and they would like, when they upgrade distances, the UN codes to remain unchanged. You can ignore this script if you are not a client that adds or uses their own UN Codes.\"},{\"id\":\"792789183\",\"title\":\"How can I retrieve the VesID of a standard port?\",\"text\":\"You can retrieve the VesID of a standard port via 2 methods: Navigate to https://www.veslink.com/distances/distancerouteservice.asmx > GetPortByName , and retrieve the Veson ID of the port via API: [image] Build a Report Designer report in the Analytics module that includes the Ports data table; the respective VesID of each port can be found under the Veson ID column. [image]\"},{\"id\":\"868122625\",\"title\":\"How do I add a new Standard Port to the Port list and Distances?\",\"text\":\"Clients will need to submit a case to Veson Nautical Support through the Help Center with the below information when requesting to add a new port: Port Name: Port Country: Latitude: Longitude: GMT Offset: UN Code: Scrubbers Allowed: Open, Closed, Not Allowed Open : Scrubber discharges wash water into the sea Closed : Scrubber stores wash water onboard for later disposal Not Allowed : Scrubber use (open or closed) is prohibited in that area Please submit the LAT/LONG in degree-minute-second format instead of decimal format. Correct : Latitude: 25 8' 24\\\" S Longitude: 42 56' 35\\\" W Incorrect : Latitude: -45.2435 Longitude: 75.12345\"},{\"id\":\"4947640338\",\"title\":\"IMOS – ECA Bohai Sea Scrubber Restriction\",\"text\":\"Overview As of March 2026, a new ECA fuel zone has been introduced in IMOS: ECA - BOHAI SEA - SCRUBBER RESTRICTION . [image] This zone addresses the regulatory requirement that prohibits scrubber use within the Bohai Sea, which differs from the broader ECA - China zone where scrubbers remain permitted. This article explains the background, how the new zone works, how to configure it, and how it interacts with the existing ECA - China fuel zone. Background The Bohai Sea is geographically nested within the broader ECA - China zone. However, unlike ECA - China, the Bohai Sea enforces a specific ban on open-loop scrubber discharge. Previously, when ECA - China and ECA - Bohai Sea were both active, ECA - China would take precedence, which meant scrubber-fitted vessels were incorrectly permitted to burn high-sulfur fuel (IFO) in an area where that is not allowed. This led to inaccurate voyage calculations for operators running open-loop scrubber-fitted vessels in Chinese waters. The new ECA - BOHAI SEA - SCRUBBER RESTRICTION zone resolves this by allowing both zones to be active simultaneously, with the most specific zone taking precedence. What Changed A new ECA zone, ECA - BOHAI SEA - SCRUBBER RESTRICTION , has been added to the Distances database. This zone can now be active simultaneously with ECA - CHINA, without one overriding the other. The zone is pre-configured with Closed Loop as the value in the Allowed Scrubbers field in the Ports List. The old ECA - BOHAI SEA fuel zone can now be marked Inactive if you are enabling the new Scrubber Restriction zone in your Fuel Zone setup. How It Works When a scrubber-fitted vessel enters the Bohai Sea ECA area, the system evaluates the zone settings independently from ECA - China. The behavior depends on the vessel's scrubber type: Vessel Scrubber Type Behavior in ECA - Bohai Sea Closed Loop Scrubber use is permitted. Fuel zone settings follow the Scrubber tab. Open Loop Scrubber use is not permitted . The system automatically switches to the Non-Scrubber tab of the designated Fuel Zone setting. No Scrubber Standard low-sulfur fuel consumption applies. Example scenario (tested and confirmed): An open-loop scrubber-fitted vessel carrying IFO and MGO, travelling from Fancheng to Chingtang: Burns IFO while transiting ECA - China (scrubbers permitted). Switches to MGO upon entering ECA - Bohai Sea (scrubbers not permitted; system falls back to Non-Scrubber tab). Setup Instructions Navigate to Data Center > Ports and Areas > Fuel Zones . Locate or create your Fuel Zone Set. Ensure CFGEnableScrubberType is enabled (this activates the Scrubber / Non-Scrubber tabs). Find the ECA - BOHAI SEA - SCRUBBER RESTRICTION zone in the list and configure: Scrubber tab : Enter the fuel types applicable for closed-loop scrubber vessels (e.g. IFO). Non-Scrubber tab : Enter the compliant low-sulfur fuel type (e.g. MGO). Confirm that Closed Loop is set as the Allowed Scrubbers value for Bohai Sea ports in the Ports List. If you have previously been using the old ECA - BOHAI SEA zone, you may now mark it as Inactive to avoid duplication. Note: Do not deactivate ECA - CHINA. Both zones need to remain active to ensure correct simultaneous evaluation.\"},{\"id\":\"1047200729\",\"title\":\"Why are the miles between ports incorrect?\",\"text\":\"The issue is due to the incorrect miles entered within the Observed Distance column in the Activity Report of the previous port. Once you correct the observed distance for the previous port you will view the correct distance. Generally, the voyage itinerary should reflect a distance between voyage legs similar to that seen in Distances. [image] As Veslink forms get approved, values in the Observed Distance field will update the Obs Dst Column in Activity Report accordingly. The Observed Distance field is found in Noon Reports and Arrival Notice. [image] Consequently, Miles column under the Itinerary grid will be updated with total distance travelled for that leg until the vessel arrives in the next port. When the miles in between ports appear incorrect, it is due to missing/wrong observed distance input into activity reports. Once the actual observed distances are filled in correctly, the miles between ports will be corrected. [image]\"},{\"id\":\"2289270929\",\"title\":\"Why does my Itinerary show zero miles?\",\"text\":\"This typically occurs when a region has been included on the Itinerary and the previous Port is within that region. Therefore, there is no distance to travel between that Port and region as they are in the same location. An example is the Port of Taboneo and the region of &lsquo;Lowdraft - Java Sea'. &lsquo;Lowdraft - Java Sea' is an entire region and not a singular Port location. The Port of Taboneo is a Port within the region of &lsquo;Lowdraft - Java Sea'. Therefore, the miles show correctly blank on the Itinerary: [image]\"},{\"id\":\"1241350829\",\"title\":\"Why is there a difference in the miles calculation between IMOS and Veslink?\",\"text\":\"Veslink Distances does not always have the same routing preferences set as does the IMOS Estimator. When the IMOS Estimator sends the route it wants to draw to Veslink distances, it does not send the detailed route it calculated, but rather a sequence of ports and passing points. The detailed route is then recalculated by Veslink Distances, but it is not guaranteed to adhere to the same preferences. In short, the comparison between the two systems is not like-for-like. IMOS takes into account the client's settings, while Veslink uses a more general set of inputs, as it knows nothing about the client's settings in IMOS.\"}]}"